🧠 Technical Q&A Highlights

Why flatten images into vectors? A matrix is an operator, not a data point. Flattening a 32×32×3 image into a 3072×1 column vector lets it be represented as a point in high-dimensional space, which is what ML algorithms need to work with.

Weight matrix sizing has no "wiggle room" - given a 3072×1 input and a desired 10×1 output (one score per class), the weight matrix size (10×3072) is fully determined.

How many training images do you need? No formula exists; it's dataset/complexity-dependent. Best approach: map your problem to the closest one in existing literature and extrapolate from there.

Local minima & saddle points - with standard gradient descent (first-order methods), you can't know if you've hit a global minimum, a local minimum, or a saddle point; you just observe convergence (near-zero gradient) and move on. Under common assumptions, local minima tend to be "roughly equal" in quality. Different weight initializations can help you land in different minima, but there's no guarantee.

Second-order methods (using the Hessian) could distinguish minima from saddle points, but are computationally infeasible at scale (a billion-parameter model implies a Hessian of ~10^18 entries).

Why guard the test set carefully? Repeatedly checking performance on the test set and adjusting hyperparameters causes the hyperparameters (not the weights) to overfit to that test set, undermining its use as a proxy for real-world generalization. Use validation/cross-validation folds for hyperparameter tuning instead, and touch the test set sparingly.

Spatial information lost in flattening - fully-connected linear layers don't need spatial structure explicitly; they can in principle learn to ignore irrelevant pixel relationships, just inefficiently. CNNs (covered in Weeks 3-4) address this more efficiently.

