🧠 What We Covered: Gradient Descent Meets Backpropagation
This was the core of the session, walked through on the whiteboard:

Why non-linearities matter: Without them, stacked linear layers collapse into a single linear transformation, the network can't learn complex functions.

Forward propagation: Traced a 5-layer network (linear layer, non-linearity, linear layer, non-linearity, output), introducing the term "forward prop."

Why loss must be scalar: You can't compare two loss vectors to know which is better, so loss always collapses to a single number.

Gradient vs. Jacobian:

Gradient: derivative of a scalar (loss) with respect to a vector, a row vector with one entry per input dimension.

Jacobian: derivative of a vector output with respect to a vector input, a full matrix of partial derivatives.

Why backprop is necessary: Loss isn't a direct function of early-layer weights (like W3), so you can't differentiate directly. You chain-rule backward through the network, layer by layer, multiplying local Jacobians with the gradient passed from the next layer, to get the gradient needed to update each layer's weights.

Worked example: Manually derived the Jacobian for a fully-connected layer, showing most terms vanish except the one tied directly to each weight (e.g. ∂M1/∂N1 = W11).

Forward pass must happen before backward pass, since intermediate activations are needed to compute local gradients.

