🧠 What We Covered: Transfer Learning, Embeddings & RAG

Transfer learning is the norm, not the exception: Almost no one trains from scratch. You start with a pre-trained backbone (ResNet, DINOv3, ViT, etc.), strip the final classification layer, and swap in a new linear layer sized to your task.

Fine-tuning best practices: Use a much lower learning rate (1/10th to 1/100th of the original) to avoid destroying pre-trained knowledge, and always match the original model's input normalization.

Which layers to fine-tune depends on how much data you have and how similar it is to the original training distribution — little data, similar domain, just tune the last layer; more data or a different domain, tune more of the network.

Embeddings: A lower-dimensional vector representation of a data point (image, document, etc.), the foundation for RAG.

RAG pipeline: Embed your documents, embed the incoming query, retrieve nearest neighbors by similarity, stuff the retrieved chunks into an LLM's context, and generate the answer. Retrieval is deterministic; generation (the LLM part) is not.

Chunking matters: Long documents need to be broken into overlapping chunks (sliding window) so an LLM can process them within its context limit.

💬 Discussion Highlights

MSE vs. cross-entropy: Cross-entropy is for classification (discrete outputs); MSE is better for continuous/regression outputs, since forcing continuous values into "buckets" for cross-entropy is coarse and wastes parameters.

Finding financial data in long reports: Use semantic search with chunking, either via an LLM classifying each chunk, or by embedding manually-labeled examples and finding nearby chunks in embedding space.

Choosing embedding models: Check leaderboards like MTEB — smaller open models (e.g. Qwen-based, ZBench, Voyage) can run locally and are often "good enough."

RAG reliability for high-stakes domains (finance/legal): Retrieval is deterministic, but generation isn't — critical applications still need human verification and pinpoint references.

Detecting model mistakes without ground truth (e.g. autonomous driving): driver engagement/intervention, high variance across multiple sensors or models disagreeing, and hard-coded physical constraints (e.g. "nothing appears above the horizon line").

Why non-linearities work (ReLU): A "V" shape isn't a straight line — stacking many of these lets the network carve out arbitrarily complex decision boundaries.

Leaky ReLU / SwiGLU: Letting a small negative gradient pass through helps prevent vanishing gradients, especially relevant in GANs and modern transformer architectures.

GAN inference: Vanilla GANs map random noise to any valid output; to get specific, controlled outputs, you need a conditional GAN with an encoded condition vector as additional input.

