What We Covered: Self-Supervised Learning & Embeddings

The "cake" analogy: Supervised learning is the icing, self-supervised/unsupervised learning is the cake, and reinforcement learning is the cherry on top, most useful signal in the world is unlabeled.

The manifold concept: High-dimensional data mostly consists of noise; meaningful data points lie on a tiny "manifold" within that space. Unsupervised learning tries to untangle and represent this manifold in lower dimensions.

Properties of good embeddings: Distance should reflect semantic similarity, mappability, smoothness (meaningful interpolation), and invariance to irrelevant transformations.

Autoencoders: Train an encoder-decoder to reconstruct the input, then discard the decoder. The compressed bottleneck vector becomes your embedding, no labels required.

Siamese networks & contrastive loss: Pull same-class embeddings close, push different-class embeddings apart by a margin M, with hard negative mining to focus training where it matters most.

Real-world example — Face ID: Since enrollment can't be a fixed classification problem, Apple uses embedding-based verification with a time-of-flight depth sensor, comparing new face vectors to a reference via a threshold.

Visualizing embeddings: PCA, UMAP, and t-SNE offer partial insight, but the real test of a good embedding is downstream task performance.

💬 Discussion Highlights

Batching Siamese training: Pairs are forward-propagated through a shared network and batched together for efficient backprop.

Search/retrieval (BM25 vs. semantic search): Which method to use depends on your eval set, there's no universal answer, and re-rankers matter for production RAG.

What LLMs actually do: Only two inputs exist, context and prompt. Retrieval and re-ranking decide what goes into context; the prompt shapes how it's used.

iPhone Face ID: Uses active time-of-flight sensing (laser + phase difference), not passive infrared, so it works in both bright light and darkness.

