Recap: Weekly Session - Week 5 | RNNs & Sequence Models



Hi @everyone ! Great session today, thanks for showing up and staying engaged. Here's what we covered.



What We Covered: RNNs & Sequence Models

Why sequence models matter: Text, speech, video, and time series data all have an inherent sequential structure that regular one-to-one networks can't capture.

Types of RNN mappings: One-to-many (image captioning), many-to-one (sentiment classification), many-to-many (machine translation, video classification), depending on the problem.

The recurrence relation: An RNN takes the previous hidden state and the current input, multiplies each by its own weight matrix, sums them, and passes the result through a tanh activation to produce the next hidden state. The same weights are reused at every timestep.

Getting an output: A separate weight matrix maps the hidden state to your final output (Y), so an RNN technically has three weight matrices, not two.

Training: You "unfold" the RNN over a chunk of the sequence, compute loss on the outputs, backpropagate, average the gradients, and update the single shared set of weights.

Encoder-decoder RNNs: Two separate RNNs with different weights, one encodes input into a hidden "thought vector," the other decodes it into an output sequence (e.g. machine translation).

Why tanh: Keeps values bounded between -1 and 1 to prevent the recurrent multiplication from exploding, though this comes at the cost of vanishing gradients, a key motivation for LSTMs.

RNNs vs. LSTMs vs. Transformers: RNNs/LSTMs still get used with small data or lightweight models (e.g. multi-agent RL simulations); transformers generally perform better but need more data. Transformers avoid RNNs' core problem of compressing everything into one fixed vector.

💬 Discussion Highlights

Vanishing/exploding gradients: Confirmed as a real limitation of tanh-based RNNs, one reason the field moved to LSTMs.

Representing an image as a vector: Run it through a pre-trained backbone (e.g. ResNet) to get an embedding, then feed that vector into the RNN at each timestep. For text-aligned understanding, a CLIP embedding is a strong alternative.

Hidden state vs. memory: The hidden state is a "thought" or representation, not memory itself, the real memory lives in the trained weights that produce that hidden state.

Weight initialization: Matters less when using batch norm or similar normalization layers, but still important in plain RNNs to keep activations well-behaved.

RNNs vs. Hidden Markov Models: Conceptually related (both process sequential state), but RNNs use gradient descent and nonlinear activations, HMMs use probabilistic message passing instead.

