Recap: Weekly Session - Week 6 | Transformers & Attention

Hi @everyone ! Great session today, thanks for showing up and staying engaged. Here's what we covered.



🧠 What We Covered: Transformers & Attention

Why transformers exist: RNNs compress an entire sequence into one "thought vector," losing information over long sequences. Transformers avoid this by enhancing every token in a sequence using the full context, rather than compressing everything into one vector.

The core idea: Each input embedding (token) goes through the transformer and comes out "enhanced," carrying information from every other token in the sequence based on relevance. E.g., in "the cat sat on the mat," the embedding for "cat" ends up encoding its relationship to "sat" and "mat."

Query, Key, Value (QKV): Each token is projected into three vectors using learned weight matrices, Query (what it's looking for), Key (what it offers), and Value (its actual payload/content).

Self-attention mechanics:

Compute similarity between every Query and every Key (dot product) to get raw attention scores.

Scale and pass through softmax to get attention weights (probabilities summing to 100% per row).

Use those weights to compute a weighted sum of Value vectors, producing the enhanced output embedding.

Masking: For sequences where a token should only "see" the past (e.g. generation), future positions are masked out with very low scores.

Why the Value matrix matters: Even in the simplest case (Value = identity), it captures the "meaning" being mixed and blended across tokens. In practice, it's a learned projection giving the model flexibility.

Architecture in practice: Multi-head attention runs several of these attention mechanisms in parallel and concatenates results; add normalization and residual connections for stable training; stack many such blocks (potentially dozens or 100+ heads/layers) to build a full transformer. Modern LLMs like GPT are decoder-only, no separate encoder needed.

Representing a sentence as one vector: Use the enhanced embedding of the last token in the sequence, since it has "seen" everything before it.

Big picture: We're now in the token-based, transformer era of deep learning. Once this framework, everything (diffusion models, images, etc.) becomes a transformer with attention over tokens once enough data/compute is available.

💬 Discussion Highlights

What input embeddings are: They can come from anywhere, a tokenizer, word2vec, another neural network, even a simple one-hot vector over the vocabulary, they're just a numeric representation of input.

Encoder-decoder vs. decoder-only: The original "Attention Is All You Need" paper proposed encoder-decoder; most modern LLMs (like GPT) only use the decoder side.

