Hi @everyone! Great session today, thanks for showing up and staying engaged. Here's what we covered.



🧠 What We Covered: Generative Models

Discriminative vs. generative modeling: Discriminative models find P(Y|X), the probability of a label given data. Generative models flip this, they learn P_model(X) to match the true data distribution P_data(X), so you can sample entirely new data.

Two flavors of generative models: Explicit (VAEs, where you directly define and optimize the distribution) vs. implicit (GANs, where the model learns indirectly).

GANs explained via analogy: A generator (counterfeiter) and discriminator (police) compete in a minimax game. If the discriminator is too strong, training collapses; the goal is a Nash equilibrium where the discriminator can no longer tell real from fake (outputs ~50/50 confidence).

How GAN training actually works:

Discriminator: trained normally as a binary classifier on real vs. fake images.

Generator: labels are flipped, only generator images are used, loss is computed as if they were "real," gradients are backpropagated through the discriminator (without updating it) purely to relay a signal into the generator, which then gets updated.

Why GANs are hard to train: The discriminator only gives sparse, binary signal (real/fake), a weak gradient. Also, the generator never directly sees the dataset, it only learns indirectly through weights the discriminator has already learned from real data.

Evaluating GANs: Balancing fidelity (quality) and diversity is tricky. The standard approach uses the FID (Fréchet Inception Distance) score, comparing statistics of Inception-V3 embeddings between generated and real images.

GANs today: Largely fallen out of favor compared to diffusion models, though not fully dead (e.g. Adobe's GigaGAN in 2024).

From autoencoders to VAEs: A vanilla autoencoder's latent space isn't a real distribution, it's more like a lookup table, so randomly sampled latent vectors produce garbage. VAEs fix this by forcing the latent space to follow a known distribution (e.g. standard Gaussian) using KL divergence as a regularizing loss, making it possible to sample meaningfully.

How VAEs work in practice: The encoder outputs a mean and variance for each latent dimension rather than a fixed vector, then samples from that distribution (the "reparameterization trick") to produce Z, which is fed to the decoder.

💬 Discussion Highlights

Why do multi-head attention heads learn different things? No strict guarantee, it emerges from differing gradients and initialization (symmetry breaking), not any explicit design.

KV cache trade-offs: More GPU memory allows more caching; when memory-constrained, you trade off between caching and recomputing, a classic compute vs. bandwidth trade-off.

