Hi everyone! This was our last live session for Cohort 5, thanks for showing up and sharing such honest feedback. Here's what we covered.



📋 Course Announcement
Starting next year, the course format is changing significantly, moving from a 2-month to a ~4-month structure with more sessions, given a historically low pass rate (30–50%). This cohort is the last to run on the current format.



🧠 What We Covered: Post-Training LLMs

Why post-training is needed: A pre-trained base model only predicts the next token, it doesn't know how to follow instructions, be helpful, truthful, or harmless.

LLMs are stateless: The model itself remembers nothing between calls. Only two real inputs exist, context (conversation history) and prompt (the instruction). Any "memory" you experience comes from the application layer built around the model, not the model itself.

Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT): Train on (context, prompt, output) triplets so the model learns to produce a specific expected answer, this is "instruction tuning."

RL-based fine-tuning (RLHF): Instead of training toward one fixed answer, the model generates multiple candidate responses, humans (or other models) rank/rate them, a reward is computed, and the model is updated to produce more of the preferred outputs. This is how guardrails and alignment get shaped.

Full modern pipeline: Pre-training (predict next token on internet-scale data) → SFT (instruction following) → RL (judgment/alignment).

Emergent understanding: Even "just" predicting the next token can require deep understanding, Ilya Sutskever's example of predicting a detective novel's ending shows real comprehension can emerge from next-token prediction at scale.

Open weights vs. open source: Open weights means only the trained weights are released, not the training code, these can be base, instruction-tuned, or specialized models.

Small Language Models (SLMs) & distillation: Used to create cheaper, narrower models for specific tasks by learning from a larger LLM's outputs.

No mathematical guarantees for LLM safety: Unlike action-conditioned world models (where outcomes of actions can be mathematically bounded), LLM guardrails via RLHF or classifiers are best-effort, not guaranteed, this is part of why frontier labs spend months on red-teaming.

