Reward function design: Described as "an art, not a science", best practice is to start from a reward function that's worked elsewhere and iterate by trial and error.

SFT question masking: Arjun wasn't fully certain why the question is masked during SFT training and promised to follow up and post the answer on Circle.

Capstone guidance (Pratim): Rather than training an object detector from scratch on a tiny custom dataset, start from a pre-trained YOLO model (trained on COCO), and fine-tune it specifically to handle degraded/blurry images, watching for catastrophic forgetting on the original task.

Capstone guidance (Arnav): Building an Agentic RAG (multi-format input support) is fine even if others are doing RAG too, but Arjun emphasized a preference for projects involving actual model training/weight updates, not just API orchestration.

🗣️ Course Feedback (Open Discussion)
The group gave candid, constructive feedback:

Pacing/difficulty: Content itself is manageable (~2 hrs/week), but combining weekly assignments with the Capstone simultaneously is what creates real strain, especially for non-full-time learners.

Suggested fix: Rather than stretching the whole course to 4 months, keep weekly content as-is but add 1–2 dedicated weeks just for the Capstone at the end (strong consensus on this).

Week 2 assignment flagged as a major drop-off point, likely too steep a jump; Arjun agreed this needs rework.

Unlock structure: Several requested that assignments not be fully gated behind finishing all videos first, so students can start assignments earlier even while still working through lecture content (videos staying sequential was fine, but assignments shouldn't be as strictly gated).

Optional/lighter quizzes suggested for denser topics like CNNs to reduce load without removing content.

Positive feedback: Several praised the course's honesty, first-principles approach, and practical/theory blend, noting it stood out from generic "learn AI in 2 weeks" courses and gave real confidence in understanding "what happens under the hood."

